Tasks: write failing DLQ-routing test (Red), wrap both consumers' handleX branches in purekafka.consumer.DeadLetter (Green) — TD-2.4, ADR 0010.

Each of StockEventConsumer.run/PaymentEventConsumer.run now takes a
DeadLetter[F, String, String] parameter; the handleReserved/
handleReservationFailed/handleSettled/handleFailed calls are wrapped in
deadLetter.guard(topic, key, raw)(...) - a message that decodes fine but
fails *processing* gets bounded-retried (maxRetries=3, 200ms) then
routed to <topic>-dlq, instead of crashing the stream or being silently
dropped. Decode failures themselves are unchanged (still logged/dropped,
never retried) - out of this task's scope per spec.md.
KafkaConsumers.supervised now constructs one DeadLetter.resource per
topic (its own small producer each, self-contained per purekafka's own
convention) and threads them through.

Significant finding, not a bug - documented in both the test file and
here: once DeadLetter wraps every processing branch, it absorbs
essentially all application-level failures internally (retry-then-
succeed, or retry-then-DLQ-and-swallow) *before* they ever reach
StreamSupervisor. Confirmed empirically: re-running Phase 2's original
isolation test (built on a failing-once OrderStore decorator) after this
phase's changes landed now observes zero purekafka.stream.restarts,
because DeadLetter's own bounded retry catches the one-time failure
first. This is a strictly better real-world outcome (fewer unnecessary
whole-stream restarts for failures DeadLetter can already handle at the
message level) but it meant Phase 2's test needed rewriting: replaced
with a synthetic-stream test proving the merge-isolation property
directly (the same way purekafka's own StreamSupervisorSuite tests
restart-with-backoff), independent of whether real business logic
happens to have an exploitable crash path - which, thanks to this phase,
it mostly no longer does. The old failOnceOn test helper was removed as
dead code once superseded.

New DLQ test proves the real path end-to-end: a permanently-failing
store call on a real Testcontainers broker gets retried 3 times then
routed to inventory-events-dlq (confirmed by directly consuming that
topic and finding the poison message), while a second, healthy message
on the same topic is still processed normally afterward (stream never
got stuck or killed).

